in a previous video we talked about the  idea of ludonarrative dissonance a  situation where the story being told by  a game's mechanics disagrees with the  story being told by a games narrative  and people had a lot of interesting and  curious responses however one example  that people brought up stood out to me  because it demonstrated not a  misunderstanding of dissonance but  perhaps a misunderstanding of how games  interact with narrative structure so  let's talk about the Stanley parable  dark souls and intended play the Stanley  parable is a 2011 piece of first-person  interactive fiction that revolves around  a conflict between the player  protagonist Stanley and the game's  narrator as the player works Stanley  through the game the narrator exposes  his actions including immediately  upcoming actions that serve as  instructional when Stanley came to a set  of two of indoors the enter the dawn is  left the player chooses whether to  follow these instructions or to attempts  to subvert them in some way this was not  the correct way to the meeting room and  standing you in perfectly well Dark  Souls also published in 2011 is a  third-person role-playing game where the  player takes on the role of the chosen  undead a cursed human tasked by a vague  prophecy with rekindling the first flame  to preserve the age of fire now these  two games are all in all very different  from one another but they're ill astray  turf or our point here because they both  utilize elements of intentional  disobedience games have a dimension of  interaction that means there's not  merely intended interpretation but also  intended play the intended interaction  with a movie is pretty straightforward  the movie starts and you watch it  there are some subversive or alternative  forms of watching such as riffing a  movie or the sing-along or whatever the  hell midnight screenings of the room  count as  [Applause]  but generally speaking is pretty well  bounded this interaction in games means  that games can do something that other  media rarely can outside a very  experimental context which is to tell  the participant to do one thing while  expecting them to do another the Stanley  parable makes this process overt the  entire interaction paradigm of the game  more or less revolves around this single  principle which is what makes it so  illustrative but it's not actually a  niche type of interaction any open-world  game has a moment where the player is  told to do one thing and can then choose  to immediately turn around and go the  other direction opting instead to spend  dozens or even hundreds of hours doing  anything else but the thing that they  were asked to do some games address this  within the fiction such as the  Assassin's Creed games that suggests the  player is playing a somewhat scrambled  version of the stories events that while  Ezio may have jumped across all these  roofs he didn't necessarily do it for  five straight hours one afternoon  instead of stabbing miner Borges in the  neck  others like Final Fantasy 7 opted to  ignore the disconnect entirely operating  on the logic that it's only weird if you  call attention to it  games are constantly telling us to do  one thing and then waiting to see if we  actually do it the Stanley parable uses  this to create a conflict between the  player and the game but at the end of  the day that's just a costume put on an  otherwise ordinary interaction the  narrator is simply a disembodied  antagonist like show Dan or GLaDOS and  much like GLaDOS the narrator of the  Stanley parable tells you to do or not  do specific things while the game fully  expects you to ignore those requests  when you are done you will drop the  device in the equipment recovery in it  now those examples are all cases where a  character within the game issues  explicit instructions and it's pretty  clear that disobeying those instructions  is core to the game and possibly even  the plot doing what GLaDOS tells you to  do generally results in death but these  two operating layers still exist even in  less overt  games games are constantly telling us  through various cues how the game  expects us to behave in and interact  with its world this is encapsulated in  tutorials but also environmental  communication and reinforcing desired  play styles the Assassin's Creed games  are positively lousy with icons  indicators and things that blink and  glow to draw your eye and encourage  interaction it is a game series that  sits on the verge of screaming at you  about the many things it wants you to do  as is the case with Dark Souls the game  may simply use standard game language to  imply that you shouldn't behave in a  certain way that some NPCs you shouldn't  kill because they're your friends or  maybe just your allies or at the very  least just because they're not your  enemies these characters are friendly or  they sell you things so you should leave  them alone that the game says is the  correct way to behave but that's simply  a social contract between you and the  game simulacrum of friends and the game  absolutely intends for you to murder  every last one of them if you go on a  rampage in Dark Souls and kill every  last friendly NPC you will actually  unlock whole new side stories and  dungeons and bosses this is not simply  emergent or procedural this is intended  play if there is crafted content on the  other side of your misbehavior then it's  not actually misbehavior because it  turns out that murdering everyone is  also the correct way to behave  what a game intends for you to do  includes not only the things the fiction  of the game tells you to do but also the  broader things the game permits you to  do if there is something that the game's  author simply flat-out 100% did not want  you to experience did not want you to  engage in then it wouldn't be there even  when it comes to potential misbehavior  using the rules of the system you're  placed within externalities that they  didn't or couldn't account for like  throwing garbage at NPCs the only truly  accurate sign that the authors don't  want you doing that is that nothing  happens at all this is because even a  negative reaction even the game pushing  back against misbehavior is content this  is a big challenge when writing about  video games that the text of a game  includes not only the messages intended  and implied by the game's explicit plot  and the ideas reinforced by the  repetitive mechanical behaviors the game  utilizes but also a variety of  potentially dramatically diverging  intended postures of play a reading of  Dark Souls must encompass not just the  nominal story but also a breadth of  possibilities and how those  possibilities alter the reading this  added complexity means that games as  texts are harder to encompass but it  also means that games of the medium  provide a potentially far denser package  for meaning than other forms and I  understand why this trips people up this  idea that doing something the game is  telling you not to do is in fact what  the game wants you to do because it's  something the games authors have  accounted for and have programmed it to  respond to it's the treachery of images  but the treachery of content destined a  patina  so what do you think what's your  favorite moment in a game where it tells  you not to do something that it  ultimately intends for you to do and why  is it the genocide route in undertale  let me know in the comments  [Music]
 

